      {
        "text": "Computer scientist John McCarthy coined the term “AI” in 1955.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://computerhistory.org/profile/john-mccarthy/"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "At a 1956 Dartmouth workshop, researchers named the emerging field artificial intelligence, but disagreed about how to build intelligent machines.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://computerhistory.org/stories/chatbots-decoded/"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Machine learning is one part of AI: it studies how systems can improve their perception, decisions, or actions using experience or data.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://hai.stanford.edu/policy/brief-definitions-of-key-terms-in-ai"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Symbolic AI uses logic and rules to work with symbols; neural networks are a different approach, loosely inspired by brain structures.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://computerhistory.org/stories/chatbots-decoded/"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Narrow AI focuses on particular tasks, while artificial general intelligence describes the aim of broadly capable, context-aware machines.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://hai.stanford.edu/policy/brief-definitions-of-key-terms-in-ai"
        ]
      }
    ],
